                                Archiving and compression

1. Archiving:
--> archving means combning multiple files and directories into a single file.
--> it is used to organize data efficiently, especially dealing with backups or data transfer.

tar command: 
--> It is short for Tape ARchive
--> this command is used to create and extract archive file.
syntax:

tar <options> <archive_filename> <file or dir to be archived>

options:
-c : create archive file
-f : file name
-v : verbose mode
-t : list the contents of an archive file
-x : extract
-C : extract file to another location

du -sh : (Disk Usage) 
--> to check out size of directory
du -sh <dir_name>


To create archive file:
tar -cvf newetc.tar /etc

to extract file:
tar -xvf newetc.tar

to check contents of file:
tar -tvf newetc.tar

to extract at another location:
tar -xvf newetc.tar -C /mnt


**Compression**:
--> compression, reduces the size of file to save disk space and speed up data transfer.

--> tools for compression:
1. gzip
2. bzip2
3. xz

--> compression ratio     time

1. gzip: compression
syntax: gzip <file_name>
i/p : gzip new.tar
o/p : new.tar.gz

gunzip : decompression
syntax: gunzip <compressed_file>
i/p: gunzip new.tar.gz
o/p: new.tar

2. bzip2: compression
syntax: bzip2 <file_name>
i/p: bzip2 new.tar
o/p : new.tar.bz2

bunzip2: decompression
syntax: bunzip2 <compressed_filename>
i/p: bunzip2 new.tar.bz2
o/p: new.tar

3. xz : compression
syntax: xz <file_name>
i/p: xz new.tar
o/p: new.tar.xz

unxz : decompression
syntax: unxz <file_name>
i/p: unxz new.tar.xz
o/p: new.tar

you can use compression tools with tar command
gzip:
option: -z 
extension: .gz

tar -czvf newetc.tar.gz /etc

bzip2:
option: -j
extension: .bz2

xz:
option: -J
extension: .xz


